1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,140
And now, for you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:13,160
So, two penguins are paddling their canoe across the Sahara Desert.

3
00:00:14,380 --> 00:00:16,460
Just get that picture in your mind.

4
00:00:16,540 --> 00:00:20,540
Two penguins in a canoe paddling through the Sahara Desert.

5
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:23,840
The first penguin says to the second penguin,

6
00:00:24,240 --> 00:00:25,680
Where's your paddle?

7
00:00:25,680 --> 00:00:30,120
The second penguin says, Yeah, it sure does.

8
00:00:33,440 --> 00:00:34,160
Okay.

9
00:00:35,120 --> 00:00:38,940
Yeah, doesn't really jump right out at you, but think about it for a moment.

10
00:00:39,680 --> 00:00:40,820
Where's your paddle?

11
00:00:42,720 --> 00:00:48,000
Sahara Desert, sand, paddling, wood, sand.

12
00:00:48,660 --> 00:00:49,800
Where's your paddle?

13
00:00:50,480 --> 00:00:53,040
The second penguin says, Sure does.

14
00:00:53,040 --> 00:00:57,560
Okay, not the best one I ever did.

15
00:00:58,080 --> 00:01:00,680
But, hey, this is podcast number 31.

16
00:01:00,680 --> 00:01:00,800
on.

17
00:01:30,680 --> 00:01:33,460
Welcome back to the Scoutmaster Podcast.

18
00:01:33,680 --> 00:01:34,340
This is Clark Green.

19
00:01:34,380 --> 00:01:35,220
It's been a great summer.

20
00:01:35,800 --> 00:01:37,760
We had a great week at Scout Camp.

21
00:01:37,760 --> 00:01:43,480
Just got back from our canoe trip and ready to go for the fall.

22
00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:45,640
It's going to be a great scouting year.

23
00:01:45,880 --> 00:01:47,160
Hope you're doing as well.

24
00:01:48,200 --> 00:01:53,580
Had a couple of nice comments that I wanted to share with you about podcast number 30.

25
00:01:53,820 --> 00:01:58,540
One is from a longtime reader of the blog and listener to the podcast, Larry Geiger.

26
00:01:58,540 --> 00:02:01,580
Larry, I certainly do appreciate your thoughts.

27
00:02:02,460 --> 00:02:03,460
This is what he says.

28
00:02:03,940 --> 00:02:04,300
He says,

29
00:02:04,380 --> 00:02:07,820
The very best scouting happens when adults and Scouts are joined together in an activity

30
00:02:07,820 --> 00:02:10,780
and no one really notices who the Scouts are and who the adults are.

31
00:02:11,140 --> 00:02:16,860
This can happen on extended backpacking trips and other events that are similar to that.

32
00:02:17,540 --> 00:02:23,720
However, this is almost impossible to achieve at the normal Scout meeting and also with younger

33
00:02:23,720 --> 00:02:24,220
Scouts.

34
00:02:24,220 --> 00:02:29,620
And here is why younger Scouts will ignore youth leaders when an adult is directly inserted into

35
00:02:29,620 --> 00:02:30,260
an activity.

36
00:02:30,780 --> 00:02:33,880
They are trained over and over again in school how to defer to adult leaders.

37
00:02:34,040 --> 00:02:36,920
And that's a very sharp observation, Larry.

38
00:02:38,420 --> 00:02:40,640
I think it's one that we've seen a number of times.

39
00:02:41,460 --> 00:02:47,640
And so understanding that is a big step in supporting our youth leaders by staying out of their way.

40
00:02:47,640 --> 00:02:51,200
He goes on to say,

41
00:02:51,200 --> 00:02:53,200
I remember an Appalachian trail hike.

42
00:02:53,519 --> 00:02:56,799
Each morning I would get up, eat breakfast, pack up and hike out.

43
00:02:57,119 --> 00:03:01,160
Each morning I left my son and his friends sleeping in the shelter and sometime later,

44
00:03:01,359 --> 00:03:03,519
usually before lunch, they would hike past me.

45
00:03:04,299 --> 00:03:08,820
Each day at the end of the trail, they would walk out and meet me as I wandered into camp,

46
00:03:08,959 --> 00:03:15,220
usually with several other adult leaders and checking to see if I was okay and if I needed any help.

47
00:03:15,220 --> 00:03:19,940
Once I got my tent set up and dinner going, my son would come over and say,

48
00:03:20,020 --> 00:03:20,480
Hi, Dad.

49
00:03:20,500 --> 00:03:21,280
You doing all right?

50
00:03:21,420 --> 00:03:22,700
Can I help you with your gear?

51
00:03:23,380 --> 00:03:27,480
And sitting around the campfire each evening, the Scouts would read the trail guide,

52
00:03:27,579 --> 00:03:30,040
plan the next day's hike and review the day's events.

53
00:03:30,420 --> 00:03:33,540
They could have easily done the whole thing without us.

54
00:03:34,040 --> 00:03:36,720
Well, Larry, I got to say that's a great story.

55
00:03:36,900 --> 00:03:39,100
And that is where we're aimed.

56
00:03:39,600 --> 00:03:41,300
They can do the whole thing without us.

57
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:42,900
We can be there.

58
00:03:42,900 --> 00:03:44,840
However, they're very polite.

59
00:03:45,140 --> 00:03:46,060
They'll let us participate.

60
00:03:47,900 --> 00:03:51,860
They'll let us kind of, you know, roll along just like you point out.

61
00:03:52,160 --> 00:03:54,160
But they can do the whole thing without us.

62
00:03:55,180 --> 00:03:56,400
That's our goal, isn't it?

63
00:03:56,560 --> 00:03:59,880
To make them successful enough that they can do the whole thing without us.

64
00:04:00,560 --> 00:04:01,520
Finally, Larry says,

65
00:04:01,520 --> 00:04:09,520
The transition from hurting 11-year-olds to fully self-actualized young men is a marvelous thing.

66
00:04:09,740 --> 00:04:10,800
And I've got to agree with you.

67
00:04:11,060 --> 00:04:11,940
It's the goal.

68
00:04:12,580 --> 00:04:16,000
It's what we aim at as Scout leaders and when it happens.

69
00:04:16,400 --> 00:04:18,260
It's very, very gratifying indeed.

70
00:04:18,260 --> 00:04:25,920
So, I also heard from Steve B., the Scoutmaster of Troop 68.

71
00:04:26,420 --> 00:04:39,500
And he blogs about scouting at www.melrose.org.

72
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:49,480
So, go and check out Steve B.'s blog and follow the exploits of Troop 68.

73
00:04:49,680 --> 00:04:50,120
And he says,

74
00:04:50,380 --> 00:04:51,960
A great job with the podcast.

75
00:04:52,060 --> 00:04:52,600
Keep them coming.

76
00:04:53,140 --> 00:04:54,480
Certainly appreciate that, Steve.

77
00:04:54,540 --> 00:04:54,980
You know what?

78
00:04:55,320 --> 00:04:57,360
When you sit here and you're talking to a microphone,

79
00:04:57,520 --> 00:05:02,980
what you're counting on is that people will be listening and they'll find it valuable and they'll let you know.

80
00:05:03,080 --> 00:05:04,000
So, do let me know.

81
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:07,720
You can leave a comment on the blog and you can email me,

82
00:05:07,820 --> 00:05:11,260
but we'll talk about that towards the end of the podcast because we do have a lot to do.

83
00:05:12,780 --> 00:05:20,040
And one of the reasons we have a lot to do is because I found installment number five of the future of your unit,

84
00:05:20,180 --> 00:05:22,320
whether it's a Cub Pack or a Troop or a Venture Crew.

85
00:05:22,720 --> 00:05:28,580
We started a general discussion about four or five podcasts ago about things that you need to think about

86
00:05:28,580 --> 00:05:32,920
for the future of scouting in your unit and in your community.

87
00:05:32,920 --> 00:05:35,900
And I thought we were all done at four.

88
00:05:35,980 --> 00:05:38,200
But hey, I've got five.

89
00:05:38,680 --> 00:05:45,320
And I can say almost without fear of contradiction that five is the last installment in that series.

90
00:05:45,580 --> 00:05:46,460
But you never know.

91
00:05:46,740 --> 00:05:47,180
We'll see.

92
00:05:47,960 --> 00:05:49,120
So we've got a lot to do.

93
00:05:49,200 --> 00:05:49,900
Let's get with it.

94
00:05:50,900 --> 00:05:55,260
Scout Mastership in seven minutes or less.

95
00:05:55,260 --> 00:06:09,140
Within the past couple of days, we've arrived home from our trip to Algonquin Provincial Park in Ontario, Canada.

96
00:06:09,140 --> 00:06:15,740
It's a week-long venture trip for guys who are a little bit older in the troop.

97
00:06:15,860 --> 00:06:17,000
We start at age 13.

98
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:20,640
This is a trip we've been doing now for the past eight summers.

99
00:06:21,440 --> 00:06:29,340
And we're experiencing some of the benefits of fellows who are able to go up there for three and four years running

100
00:06:29,340 --> 00:06:31,400
and gain some experience.

101
00:06:32,060 --> 00:06:39,240
And the point of this experience is to make them successful scouts, naturally.

102
00:06:39,240 --> 00:06:48,180
But one of the real points of it is for them to learn in this school of the outdoors some very complex things

103
00:06:48,180 --> 00:06:56,840
and some methods of decision-making and self-determination that they would not be able to avail themselves of otherwise.

104
00:06:56,840 --> 00:07:04,560
Every evening, we sit around the campfire and we discuss the day and we record our thoughts in a log.

105
00:07:05,280 --> 00:07:12,679
And this time, we were fortunate enough to have a recorder along, so we recorded some of these discussions.

106
00:07:12,680 --> 00:07:21,300
And I thought it would be interesting to you to hear one that I thought was particularly good

107
00:07:21,300 --> 00:07:30,340
about some of the decision-making processes that the scouts need to undertake in the course of this trip

108
00:07:30,340 --> 00:07:34,520
and a little bit about the nature of the trip itself and what they think they get out of it.

109
00:07:34,520 --> 00:07:41,400
And who you're going to be hearing, for the most part, is the crew chief of this trip.

110
00:07:41,400 --> 00:07:48,859
He's 15. He has been on two or three trips up to Algonquin before.

111
00:07:49,820 --> 00:07:55,560
And, you know, he was put in a situation where he had to make some decisions about where we would go

112
00:07:55,560 --> 00:08:00,700
and what we would do, how we would respond to changing conditions.

113
00:08:00,900 --> 00:08:05,320
And I think this is at the heart of why we do things like this in scouts.

114
00:08:05,840 --> 00:08:09,679
And it doesn't necessarily have to be in canoes in some exotic place.

115
00:08:09,680 --> 00:08:16,720
It can be that day-to-day work that you do in the way of troop and patrol meetings and outings and things.

116
00:08:17,300 --> 00:08:24,780
But I was really encouraged by his thinking and his response to some of the challenges that he came across.

117
00:08:24,900 --> 00:08:27,720
So let's listen. This will run three or four minutes.

118
00:08:28,220 --> 00:08:29,080
Let's listen in.

119
00:08:29,420 --> 00:08:33,020
You'll hear, you know, hey, it's still scouts.

120
00:08:33,020 --> 00:08:36,140
So you'll hear some input from some of the other guys on the trip.

121
00:08:36,240 --> 00:08:40,020
And you'll hear the campfire and various noises and things.

122
00:08:40,919 --> 00:08:43,079
But I think you'll get the point.

123
00:08:43,080 --> 00:08:46,880
We discussed the different situations.

124
00:08:46,880 --> 00:08:52,020
If we stayed there and if it was nice the next day or if it was really bad, stormy, windy.

125
00:08:52,900 --> 00:08:59,220
And then we decided that the worst case is the best case to predict in the situation overall.

126
00:09:00,220 --> 00:09:03,800
And we decided that this would be a better place to be stuck in that situation at.

127
00:09:03,800 --> 00:09:05,240
Because...

128
00:09:05,240 --> 00:09:06,680
We need a plan for the worst.

129
00:09:06,760 --> 00:09:08,420
Yeah, we need a plan for the worst.

130
00:09:08,500 --> 00:09:09,880
We don't need a plan for the best weather.

131
00:09:10,140 --> 00:09:15,719
It can be sunny a second and then in the next 30 seconds a huge overcast come over and it's pouring rain.

132
00:09:16,280 --> 00:09:20,240
And then you can really somewhat, like, feel the weather up here.

133
00:09:20,240 --> 00:09:22,060
If it's starting to get windy.

134
00:09:22,680 --> 00:09:23,720
If it's calming down.

135
00:09:24,100 --> 00:09:26,060
If a lot of clouds are scattered or coming over.

136
00:09:27,020 --> 00:09:27,420
You...

137
00:09:27,420 --> 00:09:29,940
After your third or fourth year.

138
00:09:30,120 --> 00:09:30,860
It's my third.

139
00:09:31,300 --> 00:09:33,460
But after your third or fourth year you start to get a feel.

140
00:09:33,760 --> 00:09:37,700
The first year you don't know at all how big the lakes are compared to the map.

141
00:09:38,400 --> 00:09:39,340
It's a lot harder than you think.

142
00:09:39,420 --> 00:09:40,520
Yeah, it's a lot harder.

143
00:09:40,720 --> 00:09:42,060
They're a lot bigger than you think.

144
00:09:42,400 --> 00:09:48,640
And then you finally get a feel for how big it is compared to you in a canoe than the map.

145
00:09:48,920 --> 00:09:49,640
Your little paddle.

146
00:09:49,640 --> 00:09:56,360
Yeah, being able to paddle a distance that you think is short but is actually fairly long.

147
00:09:56,840 --> 00:09:59,060
It's not hard to sit down and set up camp at all.

148
00:09:59,500 --> 00:10:03,699
It just, it takes up time to pack all the tents up.

149
00:10:04,120 --> 00:10:05,140
Get everything organized.

150
00:10:05,840 --> 00:10:10,880
Get all the food distributed in a easy way for packs to be all even.

151
00:10:11,720 --> 00:10:15,060
It just takes time and we have to set time apart for that stuff.

152
00:10:16,040 --> 00:10:17,540
And then food also takes time.

153
00:10:17,540 --> 00:10:18,600
Lunch is usually quick.

154
00:10:18,600 --> 00:10:20,160
And breakfast is usually quick.

155
00:10:20,980 --> 00:10:23,080
All we had to really plan for is light for dinner.

156
00:10:23,080 --> 00:10:30,340
We considered the time it would take to get to that campsite majorly.

157
00:10:30,340 --> 00:10:33,340
Because we would have to get there quick.

158
00:10:33,340 --> 00:10:37,520
Because if the weather came over, we would have to get there as fast as we can.

159
00:10:37,560 --> 00:10:39,600
Because there was probably nowhere else to stop on the way.

160
00:10:39,600 --> 00:10:48,120
And then we discussed if we did how much time it would take to set down camp there and then set up camp here.

161
00:10:48,600 --> 00:10:53,020
And then how much trouble you would have on the water since it was very windy.

162
00:10:53,020 --> 00:10:56,920
And it actually played out very nicely.

163
00:10:56,920 --> 00:10:57,600
I think.

164
00:10:58,140 --> 00:10:59,500
With, even with the wind.

165
00:11:00,280 --> 00:11:00,480
Yeah.

166
00:11:00,699 --> 00:11:01,520
I agree.

167
00:11:01,780 --> 00:11:02,100
It was.

168
00:11:02,880 --> 00:11:04,720
So you got to where you wanted to be.

169
00:11:05,140 --> 00:11:05,480
Yes.

170
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:06,280
Okay.

171
00:11:06,280 --> 00:11:16,260
And did you ask for opinion of the adults who were along?

172
00:11:16,900 --> 00:11:17,900
Not really, no.

173
00:11:18,459 --> 00:11:21,060
So you felt confident in being able to make that decision.

174
00:11:21,339 --> 00:11:21,740
Yes.

175
00:11:21,839 --> 00:11:22,500
Without us.

176
00:11:24,140 --> 00:11:24,439
Yeah.

177
00:11:24,440 --> 00:11:25,480
Well, I mean, we did have to.

178
00:11:26,060 --> 00:11:30,680
Yeah, but we basically decided with the group of kids on the adult row way.

179
00:11:31,880 --> 00:11:34,640
Well, that speaks very well of the experience that you're having.

180
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:39,260
That you've learned an awful lot from being here before.

181
00:11:41,780 --> 00:11:49,260
And you're able to make a pretty complex, take a pretty complex set of circumstances and come up with a decent decision about them.

182
00:11:51,180 --> 00:11:52,680
That's a pretty good skill.

183
00:11:52,680 --> 00:11:54,920
That's really why I like this trip.

184
00:11:55,140 --> 00:11:56,780
Because we were able to do that.

185
00:11:56,900 --> 00:11:59,280
And we're not able really to do that at our home.

186
00:11:59,500 --> 00:12:02,400
Like, consider all this stuff and be in that situation.

187
00:12:02,599 --> 00:12:06,699
Like, that you have to be thinking about it and have to make a solid decision.

188
00:12:08,620 --> 00:12:10,479
Now, why can't you do that at home?

189
00:12:10,599 --> 00:12:12,560
I mean, we can, but it's not as drastic.

190
00:12:12,959 --> 00:12:14,060
If it rains at home.

191
00:12:14,260 --> 00:12:15,500
Or if it's windy at home.

192
00:12:15,900 --> 00:12:18,300
When here, and we're canoeing, it's really...

193
00:12:18,300 --> 00:12:19,599
It's just like going up.

194
00:12:20,640 --> 00:12:21,040
Yeah.

195
00:12:21,040 --> 00:12:23,880
Not every kid has the opportunity to do this.

196
00:12:24,160 --> 00:12:25,939
That's also a reason why I like this trip.

197
00:12:26,860 --> 00:12:33,400
It gets you out of that environment of being dependent on technology.

198
00:12:33,400 --> 00:12:37,439
And I mean, I don't mean like technology because everything's technology.

199
00:12:37,579 --> 00:12:39,199
But like electricity more.

200
00:12:39,880 --> 00:12:43,020
And dependent on like big shelters and like cars.

201
00:12:43,520 --> 00:12:45,480
And that whole system.

202
00:12:45,680 --> 00:12:48,500
And being thrown out here in the wilderness in canoes.

203
00:12:48,500 --> 00:12:51,600
And being able to survive on that.

204
00:12:51,720 --> 00:12:52,120
That's...

205
00:12:52,120 --> 00:12:54,400
I find that amazing that we can just...

206
00:12:54,400 --> 00:12:55,600
This is very simple.

207
00:12:56,520 --> 00:12:57,320
Packing food.

208
00:12:58,020 --> 00:12:58,980
Canoeing lake to lake.

209
00:12:59,280 --> 00:13:00,080
Sleeping at lakes.

210
00:13:00,980 --> 00:13:01,440
It's a very...

211
00:13:01,440 --> 00:13:02,620
It's a very simple trip, but...

212
00:13:04,100 --> 00:13:05,580
It's very satisfying.

213
00:13:06,700 --> 00:13:08,060
Simple doesn't define it.

214
00:13:08,060 --> 00:13:12,119
I mean, it is challenging at points.

215
00:13:12,260 --> 00:13:12,599
Extreme.

216
00:13:12,719 --> 00:13:15,959
I'm not saying it's an easy trip, but...

217
00:13:15,959 --> 00:13:17,439
It's an easy...

218
00:13:17,439 --> 00:13:18,020
The whole...

219
00:13:18,020 --> 00:13:20,939
Yeah, the concept of it is simple.

220
00:13:20,939 --> 00:13:21,619
No, no, no.

221
00:13:24,439 --> 00:13:25,979
That's what I like about this trip.

222
00:13:25,980 --> 00:13:34,040
So the scout that you heard speaking, I remember very well his first time up to Canada.

223
00:13:34,360 --> 00:13:37,720
And the challenge and the struggle that he went through when he was 13.

224
00:13:38,540 --> 00:13:43,960
And, you know, the tears and the frustration that he encountered.

225
00:13:44,440 --> 00:13:48,020
And I know it was a difficult and challenging enterprise for him.

226
00:13:48,020 --> 00:13:54,340
But because it was difficult and challenging, because he got the right kind of support from his fellow scouts

227
00:13:54,340 --> 00:14:01,000
and the two or three leaders that were along with that crew, he came back.

228
00:14:01,520 --> 00:14:06,740
And then he not only came back, but he was much more successful the next time around.

229
00:14:06,840 --> 00:14:08,480
And the third year, he was the crew chief.

230
00:14:09,140 --> 00:14:13,300
And he really shone as a leader.

231
00:14:13,440 --> 00:14:16,440
He had developed some real leadership skills.

232
00:14:16,440 --> 00:14:22,440
He developed some knowledge of the activity and the ability to meet the challenges of it.

233
00:14:22,440 --> 00:14:28,840
And I've got to say, what a wonderful, wonderful way for things to happen.

234
00:14:29,200 --> 00:14:31,740
I know this is something that you experience in your troops.

235
00:14:32,400 --> 00:14:40,640
And I just wanted to give you what I think is a great example of how scouting really works.

236
00:14:42,080 --> 00:14:46,640
And encourage you to look for opportunities to make this happen.

237
00:14:46,640 --> 00:15:16,620
Thank you.

238
00:15:16,640 --> 00:15:46,620
Thank you.

239
00:15:46,620 --> 00:15:48,620
Thank you.

240
00:15:48,620 --> 00:16:16,600
Thank you.

241
00:16:16,620 --> 00:16:18,620
Thank you.

242
00:16:18,620 --> 00:16:22,620
Thank you.

243
00:16:22,620 --> 00:16:24,620
Thank you.

244
00:16:24,620 --> 00:16:25,620
Thank you.

245
00:16:25,620 --> 00:16:26,620
Thank you.

